# PART 02 — ACADEMIC IDENTITY AND LEVEL ARCHITECTURE

## 2.1 Main CEFR levels

Use the six main CEFR levels:

- A1
- A2
- B1
- B2
- C1
- C2

Support Pre-A1 as an optional introductory level.

The CEFR remains the official reference for overall language proficiency.

## 2.2 Mandatory three-stage subdivision

Every main level MUST contain exactly three internal learning stages.

The platform's internal progression system is:

A1:
- A1.1
- A1.2
- A1.3

A2:
- A2.1
- A2.2
- A2.3

B1:
- B1.1
- B1.2
- B1.3

B2:
- B2.1
- B2.2
- B2.3

C1:
- C1.1
- C1.2
- C1.3

C2:
- C2.1
- C2.2
- C2.3

Total: 18 internal learning stages.

This three-stage subdivision is an internal platform curriculum structure, not an official CEFR classification.

Do not confuse internal sublevels with official CEFR proficiency certifications.

The three-stage structure must remain consistent throughout the entire application.

## 2.3 Curriculum hierarchy

Use the following academic hierarchy:

Spanish Academy
→ Academic Program
→ Learning Track
→ CEFR Main Level
→ Internal Stage
→ Course
→ Module
→ Unit
→ Lesson
→ Learning Activity
→ Assessment

Example:

Spanish Academy
→ General Spanish Program
→ Grammar Track
→ A1
→ A1.1
→ Foundations of Spanish Grammar
→ Articles and Nouns
→ Definite Articles
→ HTML Lesson
→ Interactive Exercise
→ Unit Quiz

Every object must have a stable identifier and independently manageable metadata.

## 2.4 Stage progression

Each internal stage must have:

- A clear learning purpose.
- Entry requirements.
- Learning outcomes.
- Grammar coverage.
- Vocabulary coverage.
- Reading expectations.
- Relevant communicative functions.
- Suggested learning activities.
- Assessment criteria.
- Workload estimates.
- Completion requirements.

The three stages must represent meaningful educational progression.

Do not divide content into arbitrary thirds.

For example:

A1.1 should introduce the language foundations.

A1.2 should expand the learner's ability to construct and understand everyday language.

A1.3 should consolidate the beginner foundation and prepare the learner for A2.

Apply the same progressive principle at every level.

Allow curriculum administrators to modify individual stage contents without changing the overall three-stage structure.

## 2.5 CEFR alignment

Use the CEFR Companion Volume and Instituto Cervantes Plan Curricular as major curriculum references.

Build learning outcomes around:

- Reception.
- Production.
- Interaction.
- Mediation.
- Linguistic competence.
- Sociolinguistic competence.
- Pragmatic competence.
- Phonological competence.
- Online interaction.
- Plurilingual and pluricultural competence.

Use measurable Can-Do statements.

Do not treat grammar completion as proof of overall language proficiency.

A learner may complete a grammar course without demonstrating equivalent speaking, listening, or writing competence.

Display this distinction clearly.

